Name the responder that receives a key, because the keys arrive and nothing happens.

iTerm2 3.5.14 receives every key press, its window is key, its key equivalents work, and typing
inserts nothing; 3.4.23 in the same rig types normally. No instrument in the tree could tell a
responder that ignores keyDown: from a responder that is the wrong object entirely, so this names
the receiver at the moment of delivery and names who took first responder on the way there.

A REFUSED becomeFirstResponder is the case worth seeing: makeFirstResponder: falls back to the
window itself, an NSWindow ignores keyDown:, and the result looks exactly like a key that was never
delivered.

Under the existing CIDER_TRACE_KEYS switch, so it costs a getenv per key when off.

Task #239.

--- a/AppKit/NSWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindow.m
@@ -2254,8 +2254,15 @@
     _firstResponder = responder;
 
     if ([_firstResponder becomeFirstResponder]) {
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KEYS") != NULL)
+            NSLog(@"cider-keys firstResponder=%@ window=%@", [responder class], [self title]);
         return YES;
     } else {
+        /* A REFUSED becomeFirstResponder LEAVES THE WINDOW ITSELF HOLDING THE KEYBOARD, and an
+         * NSWindow ignores keyDown:, so the keys arrive and nothing happens. Task #239. */
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KEYS") != NULL)
+            NSLog(@"cider-keys firstResponder=REFUSED by=%@ window=%@", [responder class],
+                    [self title]);
         _firstResponder = self;
         return NO;
     }
@@ -3345,6 +3352,13 @@
         break;
 
     case NSKeyDown:
+        /* WHO ACTUALLY GETS THE KEY, which is the one fact that separates an application that types
+         * from one that does not. iTerm2 3.5 receives every key and inserts nothing, and no other
+         * instrument can tell a responder that ignores keyDown: from a responder that is the wrong
+         * object entirely. Task #239. */
+        if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KEYS") != NULL)
+            NSLog(@"cider-keys keyDown responder=%@ window=%@ chars=%@", [_firstResponder class],
+                    [self title], [event charactersIgnoringModifiers]);
         [_firstResponder keyDown: event];
         break;
 
